# Angular Data Binding — Interpolation, Property, Event, Two-way

## 1. What is it?

Data binding is how Angular keeps the component class (TypeScript) and the template (HTML) automatically in sync, without you manually touching the DOM. Angular has four binding forms:


- **Interpolation** — `{{ expression }}` — component → view.
- **Property binding** — `[property]="expression"` — component → view.
- **Event binding** — `(event)="handler($event)"` — view → component.
- **Two-way binding** — `[(ngModel)]="property"` — component ↔ view, both directions at once.

## 2. Why was it introduced?

Before frameworks like Angular, keeping the UI in sync with application state meant manually querying the DOM (`document.getElementById(...)`) and writing to it every time data changed, and manually attaching/removing event listeners to read user input back into your data. This was verbose, easy to get out of sync, and a common source of bugs — you'd update a variable in JS but forget to update the three places in the DOM that displayed it.

**Problem without it:** state and UI drift apart because syncing them is a manual, imperative, error-prone chore repeated everywhere in the codebase.

**What it solves:** Angular's binding syntax lets you *declare* the relationship between a template and a component property once — `{{ count }}`, `[disabled]="isDisabled"`, `(click)="save()"` — and Angular's change detection keeps them in sync automatically, in both directions where needed. You stop writing DOM manipulation code entirely for the common case.

## 3. How does it work?

**Interpolation — `{{ expression }}`**

- One-way: component → view only.
- Used for text content (and rarely, attribute-position string interpolation).
- The expression is evaluated on every change detection cycle and the result is stringified into the DOM.

**Property binding — `[property]="expression"`**

- One-way: component → view only.
- Binds to the target's actual **DOM property** (a JavaScript object property, e.g. `inputElement.disabled`), not the HTML attribute. This distinction matters: some attributes (like `disabled`, `value`, `checked`) have a corresponding DOM property that Angular sets directly, which is why `[disabled]="isDisabled"` works even though `disabled` "attribute" semantics in raw HTML are different (presence-based, not value-based).
- For things that don't have a DOM property equivalent — ARIA attributes, `colspan`, SVG attributes — you use explicit attribute binding: `[attr.colspan]="2"`.
- Special forms: `[class.active]="isActive"`, `[style.color]="color"`.

**Event binding — `(event)="handler($event)"`**

- One-way: view → component only.
- Listens for a native DOM event (`click`, `input`, `keyup`) or a custom event emitted by a child component (via `@Output()`), and calls a component method when it fires.
- `$event` gives you the native `Event` object for DOM events, or whatever value the child component's `EventEmitter` emitted for custom events.

**Two-way binding — `[(x)]="property"` ("banana in a box")**

- Combines property binding and event binding into one syntax. `[(ngModel)]="searchTerm"` is literally shorthand for:

  ```html
  <input [ngModel]="searchTerm" (ngModelChange)="searchTerm = $event" />
  ```

- Requires `FormsModule` to be imported for `ngModel` specifically (a very common "why isn't this working" gotcha — without the import, Angular throws `Can't bind to 'ngModel' since it isn't a known property`).
- You can create the same pattern on your **own** components: define `@Input() rating` and `@Output() ratingChange = new EventEmitter<number>()`. Angular's compiler recognizes the `Input`/`InputChange` naming convention and lets a parent write `[(rating)]="userRating"`, which desugars to `[rating]="userRating" (ratingChange)="userRating = $event"` exactly the same way `ngModel` does.
- (Newer Angular versions also offer `model()` — a signal-based input that generates the paired input/output automatically — as a more modern alternative to the manual `@Input`/`@Output` pair.)

## 4. Example

**Live demo (all four binding types + the performance trap below):** [https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-jtr5ecfb](https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-jtr5ecfb)

```ts
// app.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';
import { FormsModule } from '@angular/forms';
import { StarRatingComponent } from './star-rating.component';

interface Product {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [CommonModule, FormsModule, StarRatingComponent],
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./app.component.css']
})
export class AppComponent {
  // --- Interpolation + Event binding demo ---
  count = 0;
  increment(): void {
    this.count++;
  }

  // --- Property binding demo ---
  isDisabled = false;
  toggleDisabled(): void {
    this.isDisabled = !this.isDisabled;
  }

  // --- Two-way binding (ngModel) demo ---
  searchTerm = '';

  products: Product[] = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Laptop' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Mouse' },
    { id: 3, name: 'Keyboard' },
    { id: 4, name: 'Monitor' }
  ];

  // PERFORMANCE TRAP: this getter re-runs on EVERY change detection cycle,
  // not just when searchTerm changes -- every keystroke, every click
  // anywhere in this component, re-executes this filter.
  get filteredProducts(): Product[] {
    console.log('filteredProducts recalculated');
    return this.products.filter(p =>
      p.name.toLowerCase().includes(this.searchTerm.toLowerCase())
    );
  }

  // --- Custom two-way binding demo ---
  userRating = 3;
}
```

```html
<!-- app.component.html -->
<h2>1. Interpolation</h2>
<p>Button clicked {{ count }} times</p>

<h2>2. Property Binding</h2>
<button [disabled]="isDisabled">Submit</button>
<button (click)="toggleDisabled()">Toggle disabled state</button>

<h2>3. Event Binding</h2>
<button (click)="increment()">Click me</button>

<h2>4. Two-way Binding (ngModel)</h2>
<input [(ngModel)]="searchTerm" placeholder="Search products..." />
<p>Typed: {{ searchTerm }}</p>
<ul>
  <li *ngFor="let product of filteredProducts">{{ product.name }}</li>
</ul>

<h2>Custom Two-way Binding (banana in a box)</h2>
<app-star-rating [(rating)]="userRating"></app-star-rating>
<p>Current rating: {{ userRating }}</p>
```

```ts
// star-rating.component.ts — a custom two-way-bindable component
import { Component, EventEmitter, Input, Output } from '@angular/core';
import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-star-rating',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [CommonModule],
  templateUrl: './star-rating.component.html'
})
export class StarRatingComponent {
  @Input() rating = 0;
  @Output() ratingChange = new EventEmitter<number>();

  stars = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

  setRating(value: number): void {
    this.rating = value;
    this.ratingChange.emit(value);
  }
}
```

## 5. Example Explanation / Execution Flow

- **Interpolation:** `{{ count }}` is re-evaluated by Angular every change detection cycle and the text node is updated if the stringified value changed.
- **Property binding:** `[disabled]="isDisabled"` sets the actual `disabled` DOM property on the `<button>` element directly — toggling `isDisabled` in the class immediately reflects in the button's enabled/disabled state, no manual `element.disabled = ...` needed.
- **Event binding:** clicking "Click me" fires the native `click` event, which Angular has wired to call `increment()`; `count` changes, and because `count` is read via interpolation, the view updates on the next change detection pass.
- **Two-way binding:** typing in the search input fires `ngModel`'s underlying `input` event, which pushes the new value into `searchTerm` (view → component), and the `[ngModel]` property binding half immediately reflects `searchTerm` back into the input's `value` (component → view) — both directions, wired by one `[(ngModel)]` expression.
- **Custom two-way binding:** clicking a star calls `setRating(3)` inside `StarRatingComponent`, which updates its own `rating` **and** emits `ratingChange`. Because the parent wrote `[(rating)]="userRating"`, Angular's desugared event binding (`(ratingChange)="userRating = $event"`) catches that emission and updates `userRating` in `AppComponent` — the exact same mechanism `ngModel` uses, just applied to a component you wrote yourself.
- **The performance trap:** `filteredProducts` is a **getter**, and it's called from `*ngFor="let product of filteredProducts"` — every single time Angular runs a change detection pass anywhere in this component (which happens on the `increment()` click, the `toggleDisabled()` click, *and* every keystroke in the search box), `filteredProducts` runs again and re-filters the array from scratch, even if `searchTerm` never changed. This is visible directly in the console log.

## 6. When should we use it?

- **Interpolation** — displaying computed or component text content in the DOM.
- **Property binding** — setting a DOM property from component state: `disabled`, `src`, `[class.x]`, `[style.x]`, passing data into a child's `@Input()`.
- **Event binding** — responding to user interaction: clicks, form submission, keystrokes, or a child component's custom `@Output()` event.
- **Two-way binding** — simple form fields where you genuinely want the component property to always mirror the input's value with minimal boilerplate — quick prototypes, small forms, filter/search boxes, toggles. For anything with validation, cross-field logic, or many controls, Reactive Forms is usually the better choice (see the follow-up on performance below).

## 7. Advantages / Disadvantages

**Advantages**

- Declarative — the template describes *what* should be synced, not *how* to sync it.
- Removes manual DOM query/update code almost entirely.
- Two-way binding in particular collapses "read value + write value" into one line, which is genuinely convenient for small forms.

**Disadvantages**

- Two-way binding hides two operations (a property binding and an event binding) behind one syntax, which can make it less obvious what's actually happening — especially to someone debugging unexpected re-renders.
- Overusing two-way binding across a large form (many `[(ngModel)]`s) couples the template tightly to component state in a way that's harder to validate, test, and reason about compared to Reactive Forms.
- Any binding — not just two-way — that calls a method or getter in the template is a performance risk, because Angular's default change detection re-evaluates template expressions on every CD cycle, not only when their dependencies change.

## When can two-way binding hurt performance?

Two-way binding itself isn't inherently slow — the real issue is combining it with Angular's **default change detection strategy** and **template method/getter calls**, and it happens to be an easy trap to fall into with `ngModel` specifically:

- **`ngModel` fires on every keystroke**, not debounced. Each keystroke triggers Angular's change detection for the *entire component tree* rooted at that component (under the default `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default`), not just the input itself.
- If any binding elsewhere in that tree calls a **method or getter** in the template — like `*ngFor="let x of filteredProducts"` where `filteredProducts` is a getter that filters an array — that method re-runs on *every single change detection cycle*, including ones triggered by completely unrelated events (another button's click, another input's keystroke). With a large list or an expensive computation, this becomes real, measurable jank as the user types.
- **Two-way binding inside `*ngFor`** compounds this: if you have `[(ngModel)]` on an input inside each row of a long list, every keystroke in *any* row triggers change detection across the whole list (again, under default CD), not just that row — unless you explicitly opt each row component into `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` and use `trackBy` so Angular can skip unaffected rows.
- **Binding to a function call that returns a new object/array reference each time** (rather than a stable reference) can also trigger `ExpressionChangedAfterItHasBeenCheckedError` in dev mode, and forces downstream bindings depending on that reference (like `*ngFor` diffing) to redo more work than necessary.

**The fix isn't "avoid two-way binding" — it's:**

1. Use `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` on components so Angular only re-checks them when an `@Input()` reference actually changes, an event originates from within them, or an observable they're subscribed to (via the `async` pipe) emits.
2. Avoid calling methods/getters directly in templates for anything non-trivial — precompute the value into a plain property, or use a `computed()` signal / a memoized RxJS pipe (`distinctUntilChanged`, `debounceTime`) instead.
3. For high-frequency inputs like search boxes, prefer Reactive Forms with `valueChanges.pipe(debounceTime(300), distinctUntilChanged())` over raw `ngModel`, so expensive downstream work (filtering, HTTP calls) doesn't run on every keystroke.
4. Use `trackBy` with `*ngFor` so Angular doesn't tear down and recreate DOM nodes unnecessarily when the underlying array reference changes.

## 8. Common Interview Traps

- Confusing HTML **attribute** vs DOM **property** — `[disabled]` binds the property; `[attr.colspan]` binds the attribute. Not every attribute has a corresponding property (hence `attr.` bindings for ARIA attributes, `colspan`, SVG attributes).
- Thinking interpolation is bidirectional — it isn't; it's one-way, component → view, same direction as property binding, just for text content.
- Not knowing `[(ngModel)]` is literally sugar for `[ngModel]` + `(ngModelChange)` — a very common follow-up question is to ask the candidate to write out the desugared form.
- Forgetting `ngModel` requires importing `FormsModule` — a genuinely common Angular compile error for beginners.
- Blaming "two-way binding" itself for performance issues instead of identifying the real cause: default change detection re-running expensive template expressions (methods/getters) on every cycle, which two-way binding's high-frequency triggering (every keystroke) simply exposes more often.
- Not knowing how to implement a **custom** two-way binding on your own component (`@Input`/`@Output` with the `xChange` naming convention).

## 9. Interview Follow-up Questions

**Q: What does `[(ngModel)]="value"` desugar to?**
Answer: `[ngModel]="value" (ngModelChange)="value = $event"` — a property binding paired with an event binding, combined into one syntax.

**Q: How do you create a two-way binding on your own custom component?**
Answer: Define `@Input() x` and a paired `@Output() xChange = new EventEmitter<T>()`. When you update `x` internally and emit `xChange`, a parent can then use `[(x)]="someProperty"` and Angular expands it to `[x]="someProperty" (xChange)="someProperty = $event"` automatically, because Angular's compiler recognizes the `xChange` naming convention.

**Q: What's the difference between `[colspan]` and `[attr.colspan]`?**
Answer: `colspan` has no corresponding DOM property on most elements in the way `disabled` or `value` do, so `[colspan]` alone wouldn't reliably work — you need `[attr.colspan]` to explicitly set the HTML attribute itself. This distinction generally applies to attributes without a JS property equivalent: ARIA attributes, SVG attributes, `colspan`/`rowspan`.

**Q: Why can excessive two-way binding hurt performance in a large form?**
Answer: Because `ngModel` triggers change detection on every keystroke by default, and under `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default` that re-checks the whole component tree, including any methods or getters called from templates. In a large form or list, that means expensive work re-runs far more often than necessary — the fix is `OnPush` change detection, `trackBy`, and avoiding method calls in templates, or switching to debounced Reactive Forms for high-frequency inputs.

**Q: Is two-way binding compatible with Reactive Forms?**
Answer: Not directly — Reactive Forms uses `formControlName`/`[formControl]` plus explicit `valueChanges` subscriptions instead of `ngModel`. You *can* still build a two-way-binding-like custom component using `@Input`/`@Output`, independent of which form approach the parent uses.

## 10. Interview-Ready Answer

"Angular has four core binding types. Interpolation, `{{ }}`, and property binding, `[prop]`, are both one-way, component to view — interpolation is for text content, property binding sets the actual DOM property, like `[disabled]`. Event binding, `(event)`, is one-way the other direction, view to component, for reacting to clicks or custom outputs. And two-way binding, `[(ngModel)]`, is really just property binding and event binding combined into one syntax — under the hood it's `[ngModel]` plus `(ngModelChange)`. You can even build the same pattern on your own components with a paired `@Input` and `@Output` named `xChange`. Where two-way binding can hurt performance is that `ngModel` fires on every keystroke, and under Angular's default change detection that re-checks the whole component tree each time — so if a template anywhere in that tree calls a method or a getter, like filtering a list, that work re-runs on every keystroke even if it has nothing to do with the input. It's not that two-way binding is inherently slow, it's that it triggers change detection frequently, which exposes any expensive template expression elsewhere. The fix is `OnPush` change detection, avoiding method calls in templates, and for anything high-frequency like search inputs, using Reactive Forms with `debounceTime` instead of raw `ngModel`."

## 11. Quick Revision

- **What:** Interpolation `{{ }}` and property binding `[x]` — component → view. Event binding `(x)` — view → component. Two-way binding `[(x)]` — both, at once.
- **Why:** Removes manual DOM read/write code; declares the sync relationship once instead of imperatively keeping UI and state in sync by hand.
- **Problem solved:** State/UI drift and repetitive manual DOM manipulation.
- **How:** `[(ngModel)]` = `[ngModel]` + `(ngModelChange)` under the hood; same pattern buildable on custom components via `@Input()`/`@Output() xChange`.
- **Use when:** Interpolation for text; property binding for DOM properties/`@Input`s; event binding for user interaction/`@Output`s; two-way binding for small, simple forms.
- **Important:** Two-way binding's performance risk comes from triggering full-tree change detection on every keystroke under the default strategy, not from two-way binding itself — fix with `OnPush`, avoid method calls in templates, debounce high-frequency inputs.
- **Remember:** `[attr.x]` vs `[x]` — attribute binding vs property binding are not the same thing.

## Think of it as:

Interpolation and property binding are a one-way loudspeaker — component shouting state into the view. Event binding is a one-way microphone — view reporting user actions back to the component. Two-way binding is a walkie-talkie: both sides talking through the same channel, which is exactly why "banana in a box" `[( )]` looks like brackets wrapped around parentheses — property binding's brackets wrapped around event binding's parentheses.
